---
// The browser extension's privacy policy, as linked from the store listings.
// Source text: multiwarden/store/privacy-policy.md in multiwarden/clients. Keep the two in step.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { github } from "../data/platforms";
---

<Base title="Privacy policy" description="What the Multiwarden browser extension does and doesn’t do with your data.">
  <article class="container-page max-w-3xl py-16 sm:py-24">
    <p class="eyebrow">Legal</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-zinc-500">Last updated: 22 November 2026</p>

    <div class="policy mt-10 space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300">
      <p>
        Multiwarden is a browser extension that works as a client for your Bitwarden accounts. It is an unofficial
        fork of the open-source Bitwarden browser extension and is not affiliated with Bitwarden, Inc.
      </p>

      <h2>What Multiwarden handles</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your vault data</strong> (logins, passkeys, cards, identities, notes). It is downloaded from, and
          saved to, the Bitwarden server you sign in to, end-to-end encrypted, exactly as in the upstream Bitwarden
          extension. Multiwarden decrypts it locally in your browser to autofill and save credentials.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Account details</strong> (email address, server URL) for each account you sign in to, stored in
          the browser’s extension storage.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Which account you last saved to for a site.</strong> Multiwarden keeps this locally to pre-select
          the account in its save prompt. Sites are stored as one-way SHA-256 hashes, not as readable addresses.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What Multiwarden does not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          It does not send your data to the Multiwarden developers or to any third party. The only servers it talks
          to are the Bitwarden servers you choose (bitwarden.com, bitwarden.eu or your own self-hosted server), plus
          the icon service those servers provide if website icons are turned on.
        </li>
        <li>It contains no analytics, advertising or tracking code of its own.</li>
        <li>It does not sell or share data.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Data from Bitwarden’s services</h2>
      <p>
        When you use a Bitwarden-hosted server, Bitwarden, Inc. processes your account under its own privacy policy:
        <a href="https://bitwarden.com/privacy/">bitwarden.com/privacy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Removing your data</h2>
      <p>
        Signing out of an account removes its local data. Uninstalling the extension removes all locally stored
        data. Data held on a Bitwarden server is managed through that server.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>Open an issue at <a href={github.issues}>github.com/multiwarden/clients/issues</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  @reference "../styles/global.css";

  .policy h2 {
    @apply pt-6 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-zinc-900 dark:text-white;
  }
  .policy ul {
    @apply list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 marker:text-brand-500;
  }
  .policy a {
    @apply text-brand-600 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-brand-500 dark:text-brand-400;
  }
</style>
